Now, to find the form we must parse the page.
But which form should we pick if the page has several?
An earlier version always picked the first form, but the institutions
design their pages differently.
The University of Gothenburg uses JavaScript to guide the user through
the login one field at a time; the form that we must submit sits hidden
in the HTML, and it is the \emph{last} form on the page, not the first.
Chalmers University also has several forms, but there the correct one
\emph{is} the first one.
So neither \enquote{always the first} nor \enquote{always the last}
works.

What the correct form has in common across institutions is its purpose:
it is the one asking for the user's credentials.
So rather than relying on the order of the forms, we look for a form
with a password field, [[<input type="password">]].
That gives us the following selection rule\footnote{%
  Due to Christian Sattler,
  \url{https://github.com/dbosk/weblogin/issues/34}.
}:
\begin{enumerate}
\item If there is a unique password form, use it.
\item Otherwise, if there is a unique form, use it.
\item Otherwise, warn and default to the first form.
\end{enumerate}
The fallback preserves the old behaviour on pages where the rule fails
to single out a form, and the warning makes such pages visible in the
logs---the alternative, raising an exception, would break institutions
that happen to work with the first form today.

To recognize a password form we inspect the form's input elements.
[[form.inputs]] also yields [[<select>]] and [[<textarea>]] elements,
which lack the [[type]] accessor that [[lxml]] provides for
[[<input>]], so we must check the tag before the type.
<<helper functions>>=
def is_password_form(form):
  """
  Returns True if the lxml `form` contains a password input
  (`<input type="password">`), False otherwise.
  """
  return any(el.tag == "input" and el.type == "password"
             for el in form.inputs)
@

Let's check that select and textarea elements don't trip the tag guard
and that a password input is detected.
<<test functions>>=
def test_is_password_form():
  page = html.fromstring("""
    <html><body>
      <form id="search">
        <input type="text" name="q"/>
        <select name="lang"><option>en</option></select>
        <textarea name="comment"></textarea>
      </form>
      <form id="login">
        <input type="text" name="username"/>
        <input type="password" name="password"/>
      </form>
    </body></html>""")
  search, login = page.forms
  assert not ladok.is_password_form(search)
  assert ladok.is_password_form(login)
@

With that, we can implement the selection rule.
We log all forms at debug level on purpose: when yet another
institution's page defeats the rule, the log shows exactly which forms
were seen and which inputs they contain---precisely what we need in
order to extend the rule.
<<helper functions>>=
def select_login_form(response):
  """
  Selects the login form on the HTML page of the HTTP response
  `response`.

  Returns the unique password form if there is one; otherwise, the
  only form if there is only one; otherwise, the first form (with a
  warning). Returns None if the page has no forms.
  """
  doc_tree = html.fromstring(response.text)
  forms = doc_tree.forms
  logger.debug(
    "Forms found in %s:",
    weblogin._sanitize_url(response.url),
  )
  for form in forms:
    logger.debug("* %s", dict(form.inputs))

  if not forms:
    return None

  password_forms = [form for form in forms if is_password_form(form)]
  if len(password_forms) == 1:
    return password_forms[0]

  if len(forms) == 1:
    return forms[0]

  logger.warning("Page has multiple forms and no unique password "
                 "form, defaulting to the first form.")
  return forms[0]
@

Let's verify the rule on fabricated pages.
We build the responses ourselves so that the tests run without any
network access.
<<test functions>>=
def make_response_with_body(body):
  response = requests.Response()
  response.status_code = requests.codes.ok
  response.url = "https://idp.example.com/login"
  response._content = f"<html><body>{body}</body></html>".encode()
  return response
@

A page with a single form is the common case: the form is chosen no
matter what it contains.
<<test functions>>=
def test_select_login_form_single_form():
  response = make_response_with_body("""
    <form id="only"><input type="text" name="q"/></form>""")
  form = ladok.select_login_form(response)
  assert form.get("id") == "only"
@

The University of Gothenburg case: several forms where only the
\emph{last} one asks for a password.
This is the case that motivated the selection rule.
<<test functions>>=
def test_select_login_form_prefers_unique_password_form():
  response = make_response_with_body("""
    <form id="search"><input type="text" name="q"/></form>
    <form id="fake"><input type="text" name="username"/></form>
    <form id="login" style="display: none">
      <input type="text" name="username"/>
      <input type="password" name="password"/>
    </form>""")
