When no form asks for a password and there are several forms, we fall
back to the first form, but we want the warning in the logs.
The same applies when several forms ask for a password, since then the
password criterion no longer singles one out.
<<test functions>>=
def test_select_login_form_multiple_forms_warns(caplog):
  response = make_response_with_body("""
    <form id="first"><input type="text" name="q"/></form>
    <form id="second"><input type="text" name="s"/></form>""")
  form = ladok.select_login_form(response)
  assert form.get("id") == "first"
  assert "defaulting to the first form" in caplog.text

def test_select_login_form_multiple_password_forms_warns(caplog):
  response = make_response_with_body("""
    <form id="first"><input type="password" name="p1"/></form>
    <form id="second"><input type="password" name="p2"/></form>""")
  form = ladok.select_login_form(response)
  assert form.get("id") == "first"
  assert "defaulting to the first form" in caplog.text

def test_select_login_form_no_form():
  response = make_response_with_body("<p>No forms here.</p>")
  assert ladok.select_login_form(response) is None
@

In the login loop we can now select the form; a page without any form
still means that the login procedure has derailed.
<<find form>>=
form = select_login_form(ladok_response)
if form is None:
  logger.debug(
    "SSOlogin found no form in %s",
    weblogin._sanitize_url(ladok_response.url),
  )
  raise weblogin.AuthenticationError(f"Got page without any form and "
                                     f"not on LADOK: {ladok_response}")
@

Next, we want to extract the values of the form's variables---the ones
a browser would submit---and substitute our own values where needed.
An earlier version hand-rolled this by iterating over the [[<input>]]
elements and reimplementing the browser's submission rules: only the
checked radio button counts, a name that occurs several times must
yield several values (as in \cref{LadokConfirmDatasharing}), and only
the elements of \emph{this} form count.
Each of those rules was once a bug here\footnote{%
  For example, using the XPath [[//input]] instead of [[.//input]]
  picked up the username field of \emph{another} form on the same
  page, submitted it twice and triggered a 500 error from the server.
  Neither is [[form.inputs]] usable for this: it's a dictionary, so it
  collapses several inputs sharing a name into one.
}.
But [[lxml]] already implements exactly these rules:
[[form.form_values()]] returns the list of name--value pairs that a
browser would submit, the \enquote{successful} controls.
So we build the data from that, collecting the values per name in a
list so that repeated names survive.
(When [[requests]] is handed a list of values, it repeats the name for
each value, just like a browser.)
<<helper functions>>=
def form_submission_data(form, values_map=None, casefold=False):
  """
  Returns the data to submit for the lxml `form` as a dictionary
  mapping each field name to a list of values.

  The values are the ones a browser would submit
  (`form.form_values()`), plus the name and value of the form's
  submit button, if any.

  `values_map` maps field names to a value (or list of values) to use
  instead of the form's own. If `casefold` is True, the field names
  are casefolded before they're looked up in `values_map`.
  """
  values_map = values_map or {}
  data = collections.defaultdict(list)
  for name, value in form.form_values():
    data[name].append(value)

  <<add the submit button to [[data]]>>
  <<override [[data]] with values from [[values_map]]>>
  return dict(data)
@

In some cases, the form has named submit buttons and the server acts
on which one was \enquote{pressed}:
\cref{LadokConfirmDatasharing} has \enquote{Proceed} and
\enquote{Cancel}.
A browser includes only the button actually clicked among the
submitted values, so [[form.form_values()]] cannot include any of
them---we must pick one ourselves.
An earlier version looked for a button whose name contains
\enquote{proceed}, which was specific to that one page.
Instead, we now press the form's submit button whichever its name:
both [[<button>]] elements, whose type defaults to submit, and
[[<input type="submit">]] elements count.
<<helper functions>>=
def submission_buttons(form):
  """
  Yields the submit buttons of the lxml `form`: the `<button>`
  elements of type submit (the default type) and the
  `<input type="submit">` elements.
  """
  for button in form.xpath(".//button"):
    if button.get("type", "submit") == "submit":
      yield button
  for el in form.inputs:
    if el.tag == "input" and el.type == "submit":
      yield el
@

If the form has several submit buttons, we cannot know which one the
user would press, so we warn and press the first.
A button without a [[value]] attribute submits the empty string, just
like in a browser---this is what the proceed button of
\cref{LadokConfirmDatasharing} relies on.
<<add the submit button to [[data]]>>=
buttons = list(submission_buttons(form))
if buttons:
  if len(buttons) > 1:
    logger.warning("Form has multiple submit buttons, "
                   "pressing the first.")
  name = buttons[0].get("name")
  if name is not None:
    data[name].append(buttons[0].get("value") or "")
@

Finally, the substitution.
We iterate over [[form.inputs.keys()]] rather than over the collected
[[data]]: an empty password field has no value and thus doesn't appear
among the successful controls, but it's still a field we must be able
to fill.
The casefolded lookup lets the same [[vars]] dictionary match no
matter how the institution spells [[Username]].
<<override [[data]] with values from [[values_map]]>>=
for name in form.inputs.keys():
  key = name.casefold() if casefold else name
  if key in values_map:
    values = values_map[key]
    if isinstance(values, str):
      values = [values]
    data[name] = list(values)
@

Let's first check that the browser's submission rules are respected:
only the checked radio button, repeated names as several values, and
the proceed button pressed---the page in
\cref{LadokConfirmDatasharing}, in miniature.
<<test functions>>=
def test_form_submission_data_successful_controls():
  page = html.fromstring("""
    <form>
      <input type="hidden" name="opt" value="a"/>
      <input type="hidden" name="opt" value="b"/>
      <input type="radio" name="consent" value="global"/>
      <input type="radio" name="consent" value="remember" checked=""/>
      <button type="submit" name="_eventId_proceed">Accept</button>
    </form>""")
  data = ladok.form_submission_data(page.forms[0])
  assert data["opt"] == ["a", "b"]
  assert data["consent"] == ["remember"]
  assert data["_eventId_proceed"] == [""]
@

Next, the substitution: credentials must land in fields whose names
match only up to case, and an empty password field must be fillable
even though it contributes no successful control.
<<test functions>>=
def test_form_submission_data_substitutes_vars():
  page = html.fromstring("""
    <form>
      <input type="text" name="Username"/>
      <input type="password" name="Password"/>
    </form>""")
  data = ladok.form_submission_data(page.forms[0],
                                    values_map={"username": "alice",
                                                "password": "secret"},
                                    casefold=True)
  assert data["Username"] == ["alice"]
  assert data["Password"] == ["secret"]
@

Finally, the buttons: a [[<button>]] of type button is not a submit
button, and several submit buttons yield a warning.
<<test functions>>=
def test_submission_buttons():
  page = html.fromstring("""
    <form>
      <input type="text" name="q"/>
      <button name="proceed">Proceed</button>
      <button type="button" name="cancel">Cancel</button>
      <input type="submit" name="ok" value="OK"/>
    </form>""")
  buttons = list(ladok.submission_buttons(page.forms[0]))
  assert [button.get("name") for button in buttons] == ["proceed", "ok"]

def test_form_submission_data_warns_on_multiple_buttons(caplog):
  page = html.fromstring("""
    <form>
      <button name="proceed" value="yes">Proceed</button>
      <button name="cancel" value="no">Cancel</button>
    </form>""")
  data = ladok.form_submission_data(page.forms[0])
  assert data["proceed"] == ["yes"]
  assert "cancel" not in data
  assert "multiple submit buttons" in caplog.text
@

In the login loop, the values to substitute are the ones the user gave
us in [[vars]].
<<extract data and substitute variables>>=
data = form_submission_data(form, values_map=self.__vars, casefold=True)
@

Finally, we submit the form.
We must extract the form method and the action to construct the URL for the 
request.

We also try to detect infinite loops, and in such cases, raise an exception.
In that case, we include [[data]] in the exception, as that contains all 
variables that must be set correctly, and can, thus, be used to discover which 
variables to set.
(See \cref{DiscoverVariables}.)
<<submit form>>=
<<store data to detect infinite loops>>

action_url = urllib.parse.urljoin(ladok_response.url, form.action)
logger.debug(
  "SSOlogin submitting form to %s",
  weblogin._sanitize_url(action_url),
)
ladok_response = session.request(form.method, action_url, data=data)

<<if we're in a loop>>:
  err = weblogin.AuthenticationError(f"infinite loop for "
                                     f"URL: {action_url}\n"
                                     f"data: {data}")
  err.variables = data
  raise err
@

To detect infinite loops, we check if the URL is the same as any previous URL.
To be able to detect longer loops, rather than just the previous URL being the 
same, we store the data in a dictionary with the URLs as keys.
However, in some cases, the same URL will occur more than once,
even if there is no infinite loop.
For example, the page in \cref{LadokConfirmDatasharing} will be followed by the 
page in \cref{LadokRedirectPost}---both will have the same URL.
However, as we can see, the contents will differ, particularly the inputs in 
the forms.
Whereas when we have an infinite loop (for example, when we use the wrong 
password), the contents will be the same.
So we'll use this fact.
Note that we must record the inputs of the form we actually
\emph{selected}---on a page with several forms, comparing the first
form while submitting another would make the loop check watch the
wrong form.
<<init structure to detect infinite loops>>=
prev = {}
<<store data to detect infinite loops>>=
prev[ladok_response.url] = form.inputs.keys()
@ Once we've made the request, we'll have a new response in [[ladok_response]].
So we can select the form of the new page, the same way as before, and
perform the check.
Of course, some pages might not contain any form.
<<if we're in a loop>>=
new_form = select_login_form(ladok_response)
new_inputs = new_form.inputs.keys() if new_form is not None else None
if ladok_response.url in prev and new_inputs == prev[ladok_response.url]
@ (Note that we don't want the ending colon in the [[if]] statement, because we 
already have it in [[<<submit form>>]].)

Let's test that this works.
We'll start by providing a wrong password (username as well), so that the login 
will fail.
This way we'll get back to the same page over and over.
<<test functions>>=
@pytest.mark.live
@pytest.mark.ladok_live
def test_SSOlogin_infinite_loop():
  ls = weblogin.AutologinSession([
          ladok.SSOlogin("KTH Royal Institute of Technology",
                         vars={
                            "username": "invalid user",
                            "password": "this is the wrong password"
                          })
        ])

  try:
    <<make a request using ls>>
  except weblogin.AuthenticationError as err:
    assert "infinite loop" in str(err)
  else:
    assert False
@

